d","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-04","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Additional","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-11","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-13","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; SPECIAL","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"National pay negotiations unresolved with union rejection suggesting settlement above 3% budget assumption","lead":"Pay is the largest cost base in the council and a settlement higher than 3% would generate additional overspend pressure across all directorates.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Homelessness costs carry uncompensated burden due to housing benefit subsidy rules","lead":"An uncompensated cost burden in homelessness services, where the council bears costs that government subsidy rules do not cover, represents a structural revenue pressure driven by national policy.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Melton Local Regeneration Fund project slipping \u00a32.3m with key planning and lease dependencies unresolved","lead":"Material slippage on a major regeneration project with key uncertainties (planning, lease terms) still pending indicates delivery risk and potential for further delays or cost overruns.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","headline":"National pay negotiations unresolved with union rejection suggesting settlement above 3% budget assumption","detail":"National pay negotiations with unions are ongoing with unions having rejected the latest offer. The council's 2026/27 budget assumption is 3% pay award, but the current offer being negotiated is higher than this. This risk is rated red with unknown cost impact. If a settlement above 3% is reached, there will be additional pay cost pressure beyond budget.","category":"other","matters":"Pay is the largest cost base in the council and a settlement higher than 3% would generate additional overspend pressure across all directorates.","figures":"3% budgeted, actual offer higher (amount unknown)","quote":"National pay negotiations continue with Unions rejecting the latest offer. The Council assumption was 3%, with the current offer being higher than this.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","headline":"Homelessness costs carry uncompensated burden due to housing benefit subsidy rules","detail":"The Resources directorate is carrying a red-rated risk that housing benefits are subject to complex subsidy rules, with some homelessness-related costs not fully recoverable from government. This creates a structural budget pressure where the council bears part of the cost of homelessness services without corresponding government funding. Mitigation is limited to minimising length of stays in temporary accommodation.","category":"demand","matters":"An uncompensated cost burden in homelessness services, where the council bears costs that government subsidy rules do not cover, represents a structural revenue pressure driven by national policy.","figures":"Red-rated risk with cost range \u00a30-\u00a3100k estimated","quote":"Housing benefits are subject to complex subsidy rules, with some homelessness-related costs not fully recoverable from Government.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","headline":"Melton Local Regeneration Fund project slipping \u00a32.3m with key planning and lease dependencies unresolved","detail":"The Melton Town Centre LRF project (part of the \u00a312.2m total LRF programme) is forecasting \u00a32.3m expenditure slippage into future years. The council is undertaking affordability work, value engineering, and revisions to delivery strategy following contractor estimates exceeding the construction budget. Key project dependencies including planning matters, access arrangements, lease arrangements, and procurement approvals remain unresolved before the main construction contract can be awarded.","category":"capital","matters":"Material slippage on a major regeneration project with key uncertainties (planning, lease terms) still pending indicates delivery risk and potential for further delays or cost overruns.","figures":"\u00a32.3m Melton LRF slippage (of \u00a312.2m total project), \u00a33.2m total capital slippage across programme","quote":"key project dependencies, including planning matters, access and lease arrangements, and procurement approvals, remain to be resolved before the main construction contract can be awarded.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","headline":"Demand Responsive Transport service has unresolved \u00a30.2m funding gap with business case still under development","detail":"Public Transport DRT service is forecasting a \u00a30.2m funding gap on the council's budget. The service is reviewing mitigation options and developing a business case to reassess the assumptions underpinning the DRT service model. This is one of the four red-rated savings plans (DRT contributions assumed additional external funding that has not materialised). No resolution is expected by Q1 forecast date and further information is promised for Q2.","category":"demand","matters":"An unresolved \u00a30.2m funding gap in a service where assumptions about external contributions have failed suggests either the service model is unaffordable or external funding commitments are not materially available.","figures":"\u00a30.2m funding gap on DRT","quote":"Public Transport is currently forecasting a funding gap of \u00a30.2m on the DRT service. The service is reviewing options to mitigate this pressure and is developing a business case to reassess the assumptions underpinning the Demand Responsive Transport (DRT) service.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","headline":"Waste services facing \u00a3190k DMR overspend from contractor facility fire and disposal costs inflation","detail":"Dry Mixed Recycling is forecasting \u00a3190k overspend due to a fire at the current contractor's facility requiring material to be diverted to an alternative processing site at higher cost. Household Waste Recycling Centre disposal costs are up \u00a3131k due to increased volumes. Green waste income is down \u00a389k due to subscription numbers being lower than budgeted despite a price increase. Services are reviewing efficiency and procurement arrangements but the pressures are material.","category":"overspend","matters":"Waste is a demand-driven service exposed to contractor market conditions, price inflation, and income volatility; this Q1 position shows multiple cost pressures that may persist.","figures":"\u00a3190k DMR overspend, \u00a3131k disposal overspend, \u00a389k green waste income shortfall","quote":"Dry Mixed Recycling (DMR) is forecast to overspend by \u00a3190k at Quarter 1 due to higher processing costs following a fire at the current contractor's facility, requiring material to be diverted to an alternative processing site.","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Rutland UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"National pay negotiations unresolved with union rejection suggesting settlement above 3% budget assumption","lead":"Pay is the largest cost base in the council and a settlement higher than 3% would generate additional overspend pressure across all directorates.","also":["10 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Strategic Overview and Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-17","n_findings":11,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"202627 QUARTER 1 - FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE REPORT","top_paper_url":"https://rutlandcounty.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33044/33044.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":59,"news_drafts_total":3}}